Template: kerbridge-config/realm
Type: string
_Description: Kerberos realm for this deployment:
 KerBridge serves one Kerberos realm, in upper case, for example
 EXAMPLE.SITE. Six further values are derived from it -- the domain root, the
 DNS domain, the NetBIOS name, the domain controller's short name and two
 organisational units -- so this is the one answer the rest of the
 configuration is built out of.
 .
 The realm is baked into the Samba database the day the domain is provisioned,
 along with the domain SID and every filesystem ACL that carries it. It cannot
 be corrected afterwards without provisioning again.
 .
 These answers are used on first install only, to write a configuration set
 that is not there yet. No package ever edits a configuration file you already
 have: reconfiguring asks again, sees the files, and leaves them alone. Leave
 this empty and nothing at all is written, which is the right outcome for an
 unattended install with no answers to give.

Template: kerbridge-config/realm_invalid
Type: error
_Description: Invalid Kerberos realm
 A realm is a dotted domain name with no spaces in it, such as EXAMPLE.SITE.
 The value entered is not one.

Template: kerbridge-config/ldap_url
Type: string
_Description: LDAPS URL of the domain controller:
 The address KerBridge binds to, ldaps:// only -- plain LDAP and StartTLS are
 both refused where this is read, because the bind password and every password
 sync writes would otherwise cross the network in the clear.
 .
 The proposed value names a domain controller called "kerbridge" inside the
 realm above. Change the host if yours is called something else; the short name
 is taken from this URL's first label.

Template: kerbridge-config/ldap_url_invalid
Type: error
_Description: Invalid LDAPS URL
 The URL must begin with ldaps:// . Plain ldap:// and StartTLS are refused by
 KerBridge itself, so an address entered here in that form would only fail
 later.

Template: kerbridge-config/tenant_id
Type: string
_Description: Cloud IdP tenant id:
 The Microsoft Entra ID tenant whose users this deployment admits, as a UUID.
 .
 Leave this empty for a host that runs no synchronisation and serves no
 sign-ins -- an administrator's machine with kerbridge-manage on it, for
 instance. The configuration set is still written, with no identity provider
 listed in it, and a provider can be added later by editing the files.
 .
 This is not a secret. Tenant and client ids are public OIDC identifiers, and
 no KerBridge credential is ever passed through debconf.

Template: kerbridge-config/broker_api_client_id
Type: string
_Description: Application id of the broker API registration:
 The Entra application registration that the broker validates access tokens
 for -- the audience a client asks its token to be issued for.

Template: kerbridge-config/public_client_id
Type: string
_Description: Application id of the workstation client registration:
 The public Entra application registration the workstation agent signs in
 with. It is handed to clients by the broker, so a client needs no
 configuration of its own beyond the address.

Template: kerbridge-config/sync_client_id
Type: string
_Description: Application id of the synchronisation registration:
 The confidential Entra application registration kerbridge-sync reads the
 directory with. Its client secret or certificate is a file under
 /etc/kerbridge.secrets/, named in the configuration and never held here.

Template: kerbridge-config/admission_group
Type: string
Default: KerBridge Allowed On-prem Users
_Description: Name of the cloud group whose members are admitted:
 Membership of this group is what admits an identity: users outside it are not
 mirrored into the directory and every sign-in for them is refused. Nothing
 works until a group by this name exists in the tenant, so create it if you
 have not already.
 .
 The proposed name is only a suggestion -- any group will do. Once the realm is
 live, replace the name with the group's object id in
 /etc/kerbridge/idp_entra.toml: a group that is renamed or recreated can
 otherwise resolve to one you did not choose.
